WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.150
Every object above absolute zero emits infrared radiation,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:07.726
but a camera estimates apparent temperature from the radiation reaching its detector.

00:00:07.726 --> 00:00:11.920
The result depends on the surface, environment, sensor, distance, atmosphere, angle,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:12.683
and settings.

00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:17.639
Thermal contrast is the apparent temperature difference between a feature and its surroundings.

00:00:17.639 --> 00:00:20.690
Strong contrast can make patterns easy to see.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:26.028
Weak contrast can hide a real condition even when the sensor is operating correctly.

00:00:26.028 --> 00:00:30.985
Emissivity describes, at a high level, how effectively a surface emits thermal radiation.

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:34.797
High-emissivity materials often provide more dependable apparent-temperature readings than shiny,

00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:38.229
low-emissivity surfaces, which can reflect radiation from other objects.

00:00:38.229 --> 00:00:40.135
Reflections can create false impressions.

00:00:40.135 --> 00:00:45.474
Polished metal, glass, water, and other surfaces may show heat from the sky, sun,

00:00:45.474 --> 00:00:47.380
operator, equipment, or nearby objects.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.337
The hottest-looking area in a palette may not be the hottest physical object.

00:00:52.337 --> 00:00:56.150
Sensor resolution determines how many detector elements describe the scene.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:01.869
Small targets at long distance may occupy too few pixels for reliable measurement or identification.

00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:05.301
Digital enlargement cannot recreate detail the detector never captured.

00:01:05.301 --> 00:01:08.732
Measurement often needs more target pixels than simple detection.

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:13.308
A tiny feature may appear as a colored point while its reading

00:01:13.308 --> 00:01:15.214
is blended with the background.

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:19.027
Distance and focus therefore affect both recognition and reported value.

00:01:19.027 --> 00:01:22.840
Palettes and display ranges map values to colors or shades.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.653
Changing them can emphasize contrast without changing the underlying data.

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.322
Analysts should examine the scale, range, settings,

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:34.660
and original file rather than treating red as automatically dangerous or blue as safe.
